{"name":"Sunflower Field","hex":"#ea9d4f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Helianthus, so called probably from the form and color of its floral head, having the form of a large disk surrounded by yellow ray flowers.\n\nThe commonly cultivated species, Helianthus annuus, a native of America.","The commonly cultivated species, Helianthus annuus, a native of America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunflower"},{"term":"field","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A land area free of woodland, cities, and towns; an area of open country.","The open country near or belonging to a town or city."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To intercept or catch (a ball) and play it.","To be the team catching and throwing the ball, as opposed to hitting it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/field"}],"entries":[{"title":"Common sunflower","blocks":[{"text":"The common sunflower is a large annual forb in the daisy family Asteraceae. The domesticated form of common sunflower is harvested for its edible seeds, which come in two types: oil and confectionery seeds. Oilseed sunflowers are widely grown globally and represent the fourth most used vegetable oil in the world. They also are used widely as bird food or as food for livestock. In contrast, confectionery sunflower seeds are often eaten as a snack food or in baking. There also are horticultural sunflower varieties that are used as plantings in domestic gardens for aesthetics. Wild plants are known for their multiple flower heads, whereas the domestic sunflower often possesses a single large flower head atop an unbranched stem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg/330px-Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_sunflower"}],"images":[{"s